Context
  Asked to wire the evals into CI so a patch bump is verified through CI/CD
  directly. Discovered first that CI had never run at all: two workflows
  registered and active, zero runs, because both fire only on pull_request and
  no PR has ever been opened on this repo.

Considerations
  - The eval suites cannot be *executed* in CI: `claude plugin eval` is early
    access and unavailable on this account, and a DSPy arm needs a model
    endpoint and key. What CI can own is that the suites are structurally
    valid and that the regression tests pass -- so the validator checks schema
    and, less obviously, that expectations are transcript-checkable, which is
    the property that makes the suites worth running later.
  - The validator was tested against broken fixtures before being trusted. An
    unexercised checker that always prints ok is worse than no checker; that is
    the same failure this plugin exists to prevent, one level up.

Deviations
  - `claude plugin validate` is continue-on-error in the new workflow. It has
    never run in CI, so its exit status is not yet trusted to gate anything.
    The comment in the workflow says to make it fatal once a green run exists.
  - release.sh's staging path now degrades rather than refusing. That is a
    behaviour change to a bin/ script, but not to its arguments or output
    contract, so it stays patch work per RELEASING.md.

Timeline
  2026-09-28, nexus. Local `gmake checks` green: lint, 3 eval suites valid,
  7/7 proxy tests. `./bin/release.sh staging` now exits 0 with the publish
  step reported as skipped.
